# Commands that may run on the HOST although this host lacks the toolchain.
#
# Read by .claude/rediacc_hooks/guards/block_host_toolchain_run.py, through
# rediacc_ci.core.allowlist (the one BLOCKER parser) and the policy-path seam.
# Nothing else reads it.
#
# FORMAT, one entry per line, the BLOCKER line directly above it:
#     # BLOCKER: <why the host is the only place this can run, >=30 chars>
#     <hit>@<tool>
#
# `<hit>` is whatever the guard matched -- a gate key (`check:ci-release-state`),
# a script basename (`assert-edge-tag-exists.sh`) or a bare tool name (`bws`) --
# and `<tool>` is the binary it was refused for. The `@` is the separator
# because a gate key already carries colons. Both halves are required: an
# exception is for one command needing one tool, never for a tool in general.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE LEGITIMATE CATEGORY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A host-only interactive authentication ceremony that a headless container
# cannot complete: a browser handoff, a hardware key touch, a TTY the devbox
# does not have. The work genuinely cannot happen inside the box, so routing it
# there trades a missing tool for a hung command.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE ILLEGITIMATE CATEGORY, AND IT IS REFUSED MECHANICALLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "the devbox does not have it". That is not an exception, it is a bug report
# against .devcontainer/Dockerfile, and the fix is to add the tool there with an
# explicit version and rebuild the IMAGE (not the container -- "devbox remove"
# leaves the image untouched). An exception list that accepts a devbox gap turns
# every image gap into a permanent host fallback, which is the outcome the
# guard's own block message spends fourteen lines arguing against.
#
# The generic BLOCKER rules cannot catch this: "the devbox image has no aws" is
# specific, well over the thirty-character floor, and passes every one of them.
# So DEVBOX_GAP_PHRASES in the guard refuses the phrase family directly, and an
# entry carrying one is IGNORED with a note saying so rather than silently
# granting what it asked for.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMPTY IS THE CORRECT STATE, measured 2026-09-23
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All three tools this list was created alongside -- aws, bw, bws -- resolve
# their credentials from plain environment variables in every consumer in this
# tree, so none of them needs a TTY and none of them needs an entry. An empty
# list cannot make the guard vacuous: with no entries the guard refuses and
# routes exactly as it did before this file existed.
